<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lincolnshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lincolnshire is a large county in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary to the Wash. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was part of the territory of the Corieltauvi, and its northern part later formed the Anglian kingdom of Lindsey before falling under Mercia and then Danish settlement. It is mostly low-lying farmland, with the Wolds and the Fens, and its county town is Lincoln.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lincolnshire.jpg" alt="Lincolnshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1388680_Cathedral_Church_of_St_Mary,_West_Front_Lincoln_20240519_0252.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lincolnshire is a ceremonial county covering 6,959 square kilometres, the second largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It is bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to the east and several other counties to the south and west. The south-east contains part of the Fens, a naturally marshy region drained for agriculture, and the south-west is an upland region. A wide vale runs north to south from the centre of the county, and to its east the chalk hills of the Lincolnshire Wolds rise in the north-east, with a coastal plain and the Lincolnshire Marsh beyond.</p><p>The west of the vale is marked by the Lincolnshire Edge, a long escarpment, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-trent">River Trent</a> forms part of the western border. The county's main towns are Lincoln, Grimsby, Scunthorpe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and Grantham.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Roman conquest most of Lincolnshire was inhabited by the Corieltauvi, whose language would have been Common Brittonic. In the Roman period Lincoln was a major settlement known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindum-colonia">Lindum Colonia</a>, from which its name derives. In the fifth century the area was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, who established the Kingdom of Lindsey in the north of the region.</p><p>Lincoln became the centre of a diocese in 1072, and Lincoln Cathedral was built over the following centuries. The late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> were a prosperous period: wealth from the wool trade paid for grand wool churches such as St Botolph's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. The county has been described as having a comparatively quiet history, since it was predominantly rural, not heavily industrialised and faced limited external threats.</p><p>The Local Government Act 1888 established county councils for each of the three parts of Lincolnshire, Lindsey, Holland and Kesteven, which came into effect on 1 April 1889. Lincoln became an independent county borough on the same date, and Grimsby followed in 1891. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the flat landscape made the county an important base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, which built nearly seventy air bases there and gave Lincolnshire the nickname Bomber County.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lincolnshire remains predominantly rural and a long-standing agricultural area, growing wheat, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet and oilseed rape, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, cabbages and onions in the rich soils of the south. Its people are sometimes nicknamed Yellowbellies, a term most often thought to derive from the yellow facings of the uniform of the 10th Regiment of Foot, later the Lincolnshire Regiment. The folk song The Lincolnshire Poacher, dating from around 1776, serves as the county's unofficial anthem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lincolnshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Boyle (1627–1691) was a son of the Earl of Cork, and Newton (1643–1727), born into a farming family in <b>Lincolnshire</b>, became professor of mathematics at Cambridge, Master of the Royal Mint and President of the Royal Society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest is probably Percy Grainger's wax cylinder of the <b>Lincolnshire</b> singer Joseph Taylor in 1907.</blockquote><a href="/songs/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Corieltauvi occupied Leicestershire, <b>Lincolnshire</b>, Nottinghamshire and parts of the surrounding counties in the late Iron Age; the older name Coritani was corrected after an inscription naming them was found.</blockquote><a href="/state/corieltauvi">Corieltauvi</a></li><li><blockquote>The district was later settled by Danes and formed part of <b>Lincolnshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lindsey">Kingdom of Lindsey</a></li></ul></section>
